// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/analog/accept_sar.t27 -- the per-block acceptance checks of the external team's report of 2026-10-16, as an evaluator, applied to our SAR ADC blocks
; M5c of the analog emulation epic (#10486, issue #11212, night loop #11063); M5d (issue #11263) adds the transistor-level rows of the nand, nor, tgate and tracking_switch; M3e (issue #11283) adds the source-to-bulk axis that turns the tgate's
; two resistances from NOT MODELLED to PASS; M4b-q (issue #11312) gives the dynamic_comparator the devices' own charge and the body effect at 27 C (sar_comparator_q.t27), which turns its decision time from FAIL to PASS and
; its reset from NOT MODELLED to PASS; M5e (issue #11350) gives the inverter's delays into 20 fF the devices' own charge and an output node free to leave the tables' 0 to Vdd range
; (mos_psp103_inv20.t27), which turns the last row of the inverter from NOT MODELLED to PASS; M5f (issue #11378) runs the whole tracking switch on the PSP103 tables with the devices' charge
; (sar_switch_q.t27), which turns its hold step and its droop from NOT MODELLED to PASS; M5g (issue #11400) solves the dff's Q and nQ in its four stored states on the same tables (sar_dff_q.t27): six of the
; eight levels are within 1.4 uV of ngspice's and two (Q at a 1, after a capture of 1 and after a set) are 12.7 and 16.3 uV under it, past the 10 uV bar, so the dff's levels row is produced, shown with its numbers,
; and stays NOT MODELLED. The
; external team's report of 2026-10-16 lists, for the 13 blocks of the golden SAR ADC, a
; measured value and the limit that its check enforces (conditions: tt corner, 27 C, VDD 1.5 V, cells taken from the integrated converter's netlist). The team
; confirmed on 2026-10-20 that the checks and their limits may be used and published in this repository. This file holds those limits as named constants, one
; pure check function per quantity, and one function per block that MEASURES the quantity on OUR model, by `use` of the spec that holds the model (no copy), and
; applies the check to it. The report is cited as "the external team's report of 2026-10-16" and nowhere else; no person or company is named.
; -
; THE VERDICT RULE, and why it is strict. A row is one quantity of the report's check. Its verdict is PASS (our model produced the value and it is inside the limit),
; FAIL (our model produced the value and it is outside) or NOT MODELLED (our model does not produce the quantity the check enforces; nothing is compared, and the row is
; never a PASS, whatever a constant might have said: as_verdict(false, x) is NOT MODELLED for both x). A block is FAIL when any of its rows FAILs, PASS when it has
; rows and every row is PASS, and NOT MODELLED otherwise: a block whose threshold passes and whose delay is not modelled is not a pass. A FAIL is a measurement and is
; reported as measured; no model was tuned in this file to turn one into a PASS. THE CROSS-CHECK RULE (M5d): a row from a transistor-level model counts as produced only when the
; same cell run in ngspice-47 with the PSP103 (specs/analog/decks/accept_*.cir) agrees with our number within a bar stated before the verdict is read: 10 uV for a level, 10 percent for
; a resistance or a current. A value without an ngspice run, or that does not agree with it, is shown and is NOT MODELLED.
; -
; THE LEVEL OF EACH MODEL, and what it means for the verdict (the doc docs/analog/accept-sar-2026-10-20.md has the table with the numbers).
;   inverter           PSP103 tables of the SG13G2 NMOS (W 1.0 um) and PMOS (W 3.0 um), L 0.45 um, m 1 (the cell of psp103_inv_be_500f.cir and of the header of mos_psp103.t27), tt, 27 C, current in the log
;                      domain (M3c-2, mos_psp103.t27): its DC transfer gives the switching threshold, and (M5e, mos_psp103_inv20.t27) the same two devices into 20 fF, with the devices' own drain
;                      and source charge, the output node free to leave 0 to Vdd (an ideal gate edge kicks it 0.40 V below ground and 0.50 V over the supply), backward Euler of 1 ps, give the
;                      two delays. Before M5e the delays were NOT MODELLED: the transient was validated at 500 fF and 10 pF only, and at 20 fF the kick is 5 times further outside the tables' range than
;                      the 100 fF case that mos_psp103.t27 already says it cannot hold. Each source is on its own bulk, so the Vsb axis of M3e is read at Vsb = 0 (the 2-D read, bit for bit). A row is
;                      PASS only inside the report's limit (1 to 500 ps) AND within the bar of ngspice-47's converged run of the same cell into 20 fF (psp103_inv20_fine.cir, an ideal gate step):
;                      10 percent. The model's fall is 75.755 ps against ngspice's 75.368 ps (+0.51 percent) and its rise 93.246 ps against 92.778 ps (+0.50 percent); halving the step to 0.5 ps
;                      moves them by 0.046 and 0.039 percent. Both rows PASS, so the block PASSes. NEGATIVE CONTROLS: the same model with the charge removed (39.0 and 51.9 ps, 48 and 44 percent
;                      under ngspice's, inside the report's limit and refused by the bar) and with the output held to the tables' range (46.8 and 63.1 ps, 38 and 32 percent under) are each the
;                      model's FAIL.
;   nand, nor          two rows each. The truth table at GATE LEVEL (sar_logic.t27: a gate is a delay and a truth table, a value is 0, 1 or X) is one; the LEVELS in volts are the
;                      other, a DC solve of the two-input gate on the same PSP103 log tables as the inverter (mos_psp103_gates.t27, M5d), each level within 3 uV of ngspice's run of the
;                      same cell. Both rows PASS, so the two blocks PASS.
;   tgate              three rows on the same tables (mos_psp103_gates.t27): the off leakage (291.0 pA, 7.2 percent from ngspice's real cell), Ron at 0.75 V and the worst Ron over 0.1 to 1.4 V.
;                      The two resistances are read on the tables WITH A SOURCE-TO-BULK AXIS (M3e, mos_psp103_vsb_data.t27: seven slices, Vsb 0 to 1.5 V, each device read at the Vsb its source
;                      has with the bulks on the rails): 2461.2 ohm and 2777.5 ohm, within 0.07 and 0.05 percent of ngspice's real cell (2459.6 and 2776.1 ohm; the largest of 27 gaps is 0.09
;                      percent), inside the report's 30 and 50 kohm: all three rows PASS and the block PASSes. Before M3e the tables had no body effect and read 1718 and 2109 ohm, 30 and 24
;                      percent under the real cell, past the 10 percent bar, and the two rows were NOT MODELLED; that read is kept (body false) as the negative control and still gives NOT MODELLED.
;   dff                the gate level (inside SAR_logic): capture, hold, set, reset and the clock-to-Q delay are measured on it. THE LEVELS (M5g, sar_dff_q.t27) are a DC solve of the cell's nodes on the same tt 27 C
;                      PSP103 log tables as the nand and the nor, in the four stored states of the deck (accept_dff.cir): Q and nQ sit within 2 uV of a rail (the report's limit is 50 mV). A row is PASS only inside
;                      the report's limit AND with each of the eight within 10 uV of ngspice-47's run of the same cell. Six are, within 1.33 uV; Q's 1 level is 12.73 uV (after a capture of 1) and 16.30 uV (after a
;                      set) under ngspice's, because Q drives four gates and the tables hold no gate current: the two do not agree, so the row is NOT MODELLED, with those numbers beside it, and the block stays NOT
;                      MODELLED. NEGATIVE CONTROLS: a dead pull-up, a dead NMOS and a pull-up at a hundredth of its width are refused; THE LIMIT: a pull-up at a TENTH of its width lands within 4.07 uV of ngspice's and is
;                      ACCEPTED by the 10 uV bar (the row would be PASS for that wrong cell and is NOT MODELLED for the right one), so the bar is not loosened to make a PASS and none is claimed.
;   dynamic_comparator the model of sar_comparator_q.t27 (M4b-q): the PSP103 log tables of the two devices at 27 C, the report's temperature, read on the source-to-bulk axis (the input pair and XM8, XM9 have
;                      their source off their bulk), the devices' own drain, gate and source charge from the M3c tables, backward Euler of 1.6 ps. Before it the comparator was the NO-CHARGE
;                      model on the 50 C tables, which decided 98 times too fast (5.35 ps against the 523 ps of ngspice's golden-deck start at +2 mV) and FAILED the 10 ps lower limit. Now all four
;                      rows are produced: the sign at +-2 mV, the end levels at 2 ns, the reset levels 1.4 ns after the clock has fallen (at 2 ns), and the decision time, 613.07 ps. A row is PASS
;                      only inside the report's limit AND within the bar of ngspice-47's run of the same comparator at 27 C (the settled start, sar_cmp_q_ref.cir: the decision 602.537 ps, the levels
;                      1.49884 V and 2.283 mV at 2 ns, 3.265 mV and -0.247 mV in the reset): 10 percent for the time (it is 1.75 percent over), 5 mV for a level (0.49 mV and 0.19 mV off); a value
;                      that disagrees is the model's FAIL. All four rows PASS, so the block PASSes. NEGATIVE CONTROL: the same model with the devices' charge removed decides at 5.40 ps and FAILS.
;   tracking_switch    three rows, on two models. The tracking error at DC (1.97 uV, the arithmetic floor, 2.3 uV from ngspice's PSP103 run of the same cell) is measured on the SQUARE LAW
;                      bootstrapped switch (sar_switch.t27, MOSFET level 1), as before. The hold step and the droop were NOT MODELLED, because the square law has no gate capacitance (no charge
;                      injection) and no leakage: its 0.18 uV and 0 V would "pass" a 30 mV and a 5 mV limit by having no effect at all. M5f measures them on the whole cell on the PSP103 tables
;                      (sar_switch_q.t27: ten transistors and the inverter, the devices' own charge, the source-to-bulk axis, backward Euler of 10 ps) at the report's conditions, 0.3 V and 1.2 V into
;                      2 pF: the hold step is -1.634 and -1.893 mV against ngspice's -1.596 and -1.730 mV (+2.4 and +9.4 percent; the report's -1.6 and -1.7), inside 30 mV; the droop, the off
;                      channel's leakage over 413 ns, is +0.24 uV at 0.3 V and 0 at 1.2 V (one IUNIT, 1.164 pA, read at the tables' lowest gate row, an upper bound; ngspice's is zero at the six digits it
;                      prints, under 1 uV and under 10 uV), inside 5 mV. A row is PASS only inside the report's limit AND within 10 percent (the step) or inside ngspice's bound (the droop): a
;                      value that disagrees is the model's FAIL. All three rows PASS, so the block PASSes. NEGATIVE CONTROLS: the devices' charge removed (no step, 100 percent from ngspice's, inside
;                      the report's 30 mV) FAILS the hold row; ten times the leakage (2.4 uV, inside the report's 5 mV and 2.4 times ngspice's bound at 0.3 V) FAILS the droop row.
;   clock_gen, SAR_logic  gate level with delay tables MEASURED on the golden gates in ngspice (sar_logic.t27, 113 gates); the delays are the model's own run, within 8 % of ngspice.
;   DAC_control, DAC_control_top, neg_bottom_plate_logic  NOT MODELLED. The DAC (sar_cdac.t27) has ideal capacitors driven by ideal plate sources: the level a plate reaches
;                      is its input, not an output; the latches and the N half's follow-then-complement logic are not circuits in any spec.
;   integration        the behavioural converter (sar_adc.t27): ideal DAC capacitors plus ONE fitted top-plate parasitic and the comparator offset measured on the netlist
;                      (sar_kick.t27), on an ideal sampling input. A PASS here is a PASS of that model, which was fitted to the golden deck's 30 of 31 codes; it is not a
;                      circuit-level result.
; -
; WHAT THE ROWS ARE NOT. The conditions differ in places and each is stated in the doc: the report's tracking switch drives 2 pF at 0.3 and 1.2 V, ours drives 3.93 pF at
; 0.2 and 1.3 V (ngspice's PSP103 cell is run at both: the tracking error is under 0.4 uV at all four, and the hold step at the report's conditions is the report's; the hold step and the droop
; rows of M5f are measured at the report's own conditions, the model of sar_switch_q.t27 also at ours: -0.825 and -0.979 mV against ngspice's -0.812 and -0.890, +1.6 and +10.0 percent, context, not rows); the levels of the
; nand and nor are the DC solution, which is what a gate settles to into 10 fF, not a transient; the comparator is at 27 C as the report's is, on a 1.6 ps backward Euler grid, with the inputs held
; and the clock an ideal step, not the converter's delay-cell clock, its input stages' kickback or its NAND "Valid"; the reset level is read 1.4 ns after the clock falls
; (the report's own timing of it is not in the material this file was made from); the report's ramp for the converter has 8 conversions
; whose ideal end codes are 517 and 1579, ours is the literal -1 to +1 V ramp, and the report's own ramp is rebuilt as context only. The stop time of the clock is taken
; from the eleventh Valid rise to the last clkc fall at the golden pace of 40 ns; the report's 10.7 ns is not reproduced by that definition (ours is 5.70 ns) and its
; definition is not in the material this file was made from.
; -
; THE NEGATIVE CONTROLS (FR-004), each a test that passes by seeing the failure. Every check function refuses a broken value (a delay out of range, a swapped bit, a wrong
; code) and accepts the report's own measured value, so no check is always-FAIL or always-PASS. Where a model can be broken to leave the limit, that is done too: the
; PMOS table zeroed (the output never falls through Vdd / 2: the reader returns -0.031 V, no crossing), the inputs of the comparator exchanged (the sign flips), the comparator's charge removed (5.40 ps, refused by the 10 ps limit and by the cross-check), the boost capacitor removed (tracking error 25.4 mV, refused by the limit and by the cross-check), the trim code
; ignored (ratio 1.0), a dff with Set and Reset exchanged (the order, the counts and the set and reset rows fail), a converter whose MSB capacitor is halved (237 LSB), a gate with
; its PMOS or NMOS table zeroed (the 1 levels at 0 V, the 0 level at 1.5 V), the tgate with the NMOS table in both slots (521 pA, inside the report's 1 nA and 82 percent over ngspice's, so the cross-check refuses it), the tgate read with Vsb forced to 0 (M3e: 1718 and 2109 ohm, inside the report's limits and 30 and 24 percent under ngspice's real cell, so the cross-check refuses it and the rows are NOT MODELLED), the tracking switch with the devices' charge removed (M5f: no hold step, under 1 uV, inside the report's 30 mV and 100 percent from ngspice's, so the hold row FAILS) and with ten times the leakage (2.4 uV of droop, inside the report's 5 mV and 2.4 times the 1 uV bound of ngspice's run at 0.3 V, so the droop row FAILS), the dff with x4's pull-up dead, its NMOS dead or its pull-up at a hundredth of its width (M5g: 1.5 V, 1.5 V and 186 uV off Q's rail, refused by the report's 50 mV or by the 10 uV bar; the row stays NOT MODELLED), and, as a LIMIT and not a control, the same pull-up at a tenth of its width (Q's 1 level 18.7 uV, within 4.07 uV of ngspice's: inside the 10 uV bar, so the bar does not see it, sar_dff_q.t27).
; Honest limit of the control: a few of the report's limits are wide (the inverter threshold 0.5 to 1.0 V, a bridge of 2 units gives 45.5 LSB against 64), and a model
; error inside the limit is not seen by that check; the sar_adc.t27 tests see it by the codes. The level cross-check of a gate does not see a device's width (the two tables in each
; other's slots stay within 3 uV of ngspice's levels, mos_psp103_gates.t27 pins it); the tgate's leakage and Ron do.
; -
; THE REFERENCE RUN IS CHECKED AGAINST THE REPORT. The ngspice runs the cross-checks use give what the external team measured on the same cells: the real tgate 2459.6 ohm at 0.75 V
; (the report 2.44 kohm) and 2776.1 ohm worst (2.78 kohm), 0.2856 nA off (0.29 nA), and the PSP103 tracking switch -1.596 and -1.730 mV of hold step (-1.6 and -1.7 mV), so the
; numbers a model is judged by are the report's cells; a test pins each.
; -
; WHAT A PASS SAYS. For the clock_gen, SAR_logic, integration, nand, nor, tgate, dynamic_comparator, inverter and tracking_switch blocks: every quantity the report's check enforces is produced by our model and is inside the report's
; limit. For a row marked PASS inside a NOT MODELLED block: that quantity, at the model's level above, is inside the limit. WHAT IT DOES NOT SAY. Nothing about a transistor-level
; result for the gate-level blocks (dff, clock_gen, SAR_logic), nothing about voltage levels of any digital node but the nand's and nor's and the dff's Q and nQ (whose two 1 levels, after a capture of 1 and after a set, are 12.7 and
; 16.3 uV under ngspice's, past the bar: the tables have no gate current), nothing about the delay of any gate into a load,
; nothing about the tgate's resistance beyond the 27 outputs at 10 mV across it (the body effect is in the tables from M3e, at 27 C), nothing about the comparator's nodes between the end levels
; (up to 83 mV off ngspice's in the regeneration, 0.31 V in the reset) or its resolution time (4.9 percent early), nothing about power, noise, mismatch or another corner or temperature (the report's smoke
; suite runs five corners at -40, 27 and 125 C; our tables have no -40 or 125 C and the line is NOT MODELLED), nothing about the body effect on the switch's charge, its junctions' leakage or a changing
; input (sar_switch_q.t27: the hold step is 9.4 percent over ngspice's at 1.2 V, 0.6 point inside the bar, and 10.0 percent at the DAC's 1.3 V, which is not a row), nothing about speed. The report's numbers are the report's; ours
; are computed here by the named functions. ASCII only, English only.
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module AnalogAcceptSar;

